{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789377907,"nonce":"AWXOfiywNNJuByzsRF4Qd/EytzE=","title":"Development run: a report of what the runtime did, and tiers reached across the bridge","message":"A developer testing a two-language program should not have to fight the runtime, or wait for the AOT build, to see whether something was optimized. Today the answer is `WLIFT_TIER_STATS=1`, `ASH_PROFILE=sample` and a sampler listing naming `_tlv_get_addr`; none of it is a thing a `caribou run` offers.\n\nTwo parts.\n\n1. A run report from the driver, no environment switches, in the developer's own terms: per Wren method, interpreted / baseline / optimized; per Haxe call site into Wren and Wren site into Haxe, whether it has a direct send or takes the plain path every call; per callback, typed (record closure) or boxed (var-args); per constructor and member, which arguments cross as `Dynamic` and would stay on the bridge even at AOT (0683393), with the `#export` that would fix it. The interop bench's constructor boxed an `Int` for a whole session because nothing said so.\n\n2. Tier promotion across the bridge. A Wren method only ever called from Haxe stays baseline: `call_found_closure` never ticks wren_lift's promotion counter, since the tick (`record_call`) costs more than the call. Sampled ticks from the bridge — one in N — cost nothing per call and let a hot cross-language path reach the top tier within seconds of being hot, in a development run.\n\nBoth report on the same fixture: the Swarm arena bench (a headless mixed-language game loop), which is also where reload (092f16b) will be exercised.","files":null}]}